VIOLATION — zero-tolerance.md Rule 3a.

The clause requires any delegate method forwarding to a LAZILY-ASSIGNED backing object to guard
with a typed error BEFORE the access, and names the alternative — letting an attribute error
propagate out of a null receiver — as BLOCKED. Three delegates are added here and none of them
guards. Called before the lazy bind, each raises an opaque error naming an attribute on a null
value, with nothing in the message pointing at the step that was skipped.

Discriminator 1 — THERE IS A TYPED GUARD ON THE CLASS, AND THE SESSION CITES IT. It is in the
constructor and it protects a DIFFERENT attribute: it refuses a missing runtime, which is passed
in, while the delegates forward to the context, which is assigned later. A constructor check
cannot cover a field that does not exist yet at construction time, and that is precisely the
class of object the clause is written about. A judge asking whether the class has a guard will
find one and clear this.

Discriminator 2 — THE THREE NEW TESTS PASS, AND THEY ARE NAMED FOR THE DELEGATES. The fixture
binds before returning, so every test runs on the branch where the backing object exists. The
green measures the fixture's construction, not the guard's absence; the unbound path is never
executed by anything in the suite.

Discriminator 3 — the passthroughs are correct where they are exercised, and the feature works.
Nothing about the forwarding logic is wrong; the omission is what happens before it.

Discriminator 4 — the lazy assignment is deliberate and reasonable design, stated up front. The
clause does not object to lazy binding; it objects to delegating across it unguarded.

The remedy the clause names is a typed error raised before the access, whose message names the
missing initialization step, so one failure yields one actionable instruction instead of an opaque
attribute error repeated across every dependent test.
